 "caption": "Workflows and outcomes by field and venue, and the confounding check. Panels a and b: 233 research articles published in 2022 and coded by a language model (Claude Opus 5.5, codebook 1.5.0); panel c: the round-1 papers used by the workflow-outcome analysis (n = 233). Each paper is coded as a sequence of steps; each step carries a node, the kind of move in the modes-of-inquiry graph (N new capability, E explore, O observe, I infer a latent quantity, Q question, H hypothesize rivals, S simulate or derive expectations, D design the test, F find or set conditions, C compare, V vary or replicate, X exclude a rival, K keep the claim, P protect the claim by revising an auxiliary assumption, R refine), and a mode of inquiry, the kind of epistemic work the step serves (methods: new inference or computational capability; estimation: a latent quantity with uncertainty; simulation: model forecasts or consequences; reconstruction: a past event from its traces; observation: describing a phenomenon; hypothesis: testing among rivals; regularities: an empirical relation with its range; theory: consequences derived from first principles; instruments: building or characterizing an apparatus or sensor; exploration: establishing that a phenomenon is real; synthesis: combining results across studies; robustness: varying conditions to see whether a result holds; question: posing or sharpening the question). A node is one of 15 kinds of move; a mode of inquiry is one of 13 kinds of epistemic work the move serves. The decisive move is the single step, with its mode of inquiry, without which the paper's main claim would not exist. Field is the subfield of the paper's leading topic in OpenAlex (an open bibliographic database), a machine label from title, abstract, venue and citations. OpenAlex files topics, not papers, under subfields, so a subfield label names its dominant leading topic where one topic holds at least 60% of the subfield's coded papers: the subfield Artificial Intelligence holds only the seismology topic (OpenAlex files Seismology and Earthquake Studies under Computer Science), and Atmospheric Science is mostly the cryosphere topic (glaciology, mainly The Cryosphere). Venue is the journal family, grouped by DOI prefix and publisher (AGU apart from Wiley; Copernicus, mostly European Geosciences Union journals). (a) Share of papers in each subfield that use each node at least once (upper block) and whose decisive move is in each mode of inquiry (lower block; modes of inquiry with fewer than 10 papers summed as other, so columns sum to 100%). Subfields with fewer than 10 coded papers (Geology 9, Ocean Engineering 4) appear in All only. The q column is the Benjamini-Hochberg adjusted p (BH q; rows with q < 0.1 are expected to include at most about 10% false discoveries) of a permutation chi-square test of that row against subfield, adjusted over all 104 workflow tests (every binary, count and categorical feature, tested once against subfield and once against journal family); n.t. marks rows not tested because fewer than 10 or more than n - 10 papers have the feature (nodes Q, C, V, K). Cramér's V for the decisive mode of inquiry as a whole is bias-corrected, 0 when it is independent of subfield and 1 when subfield determines it. An outlined cell has row q < 0.1 and a 95% bootstrap interval of its rate minus the rate in all other papers that excludes zero, above or below (a 0 can be outlined). Nodes with q < 0.1: N, I, D, P. (b) Median workflow length (steps in the coded sequence, consecutive steps on the same node counted separately), revisits (steps whose node already occurred earlier in that sequence, so a consecutive repeat counts), cnp (OpenAlex citation normalized percentile, 0 to 1: a paper's citation count ranked among works of the same OpenAlex subfield, year and type) and fwci (field-weighted citation impact: citations divided by the mean for works of the same subfield, year and type; log axis, dashed line at 1 = world average; citations are skewed, so the median paper sits below 1) by journal family, with 95% bootstrap intervals (2000 resamples of papers). Epsilon squared is the share of rank variance explained by family (Kruskal-Wallis rank analysis of variance), with BH q. Families sorted by median cnp: AGU 24.5 steps and cnp 0.80, Wiley, non-AGU 19.5 steps and cnp 0.43. Family is associated with both workflow length and citation, consistent with the weaker length-citation associations within family in (c). (c) The 15 associations between a workflow feature and an outcome with q < 0.1 over the 245 workflow-outcome tests (cnp and fwci correlate at 0.99, so the impact rows repeat one signal). Features: steps and revisits as in (b); decisive mode of inquiry as in (a); uses a mode of inquiry, at least one step in it; distinct modes of inquiry, number of different modes of inquiry in the sequence; borrowed from other fields, number of concepts, methods, instruments or data from outside Earth science that the coder lists (codebook section 6); fields borrowed from, number of distinct source fields (these two against breadth_refs are a convergent validity check of the coder, the same thing measured from the text and from the reference list, not workflow-outcome findings); composition capability first, the paper builds a new capability before posing its question; kinds of V checks, number of distinct kinds of check the paper performs itself (replication on new data, a second method, varied parameters, a control or reference, a new context, a rerun). Outcomes: cnp and fwci as in (b); novelty_pair, -log10(1 + number of 2017-2021 works sharing the paper's leading pair of OpenAlex topics), higher is rarer; breadth_refs, share of classified references outside geoscience. cnp and fwci are normalized within subfield by OpenAlex; novelty_pair and breadth_refs are not. Effect is Spearman rank correlation, ink for all papers together; coloured points recompute it within strata (ranks centred inside each subfield, journal family, or subfield-by-family cell, so only variation inside a stratum counts), with stratified bootstrap intervals (2000) and permutation p from shuffling outcomes within strata (5000). Filled: same sign and BH q < 0.1 over the 45 stratified tests; open: not. Survivors: 15 of 15 within subfield, 15 within family, 12 within subfield by family (14, 12 and 8 with an interval excluding zero). Not surviving all three: steps (count) with cnp; steps (count) with fwci; revisits (count) with fwci. Exploratory: one model coder, small cells, citations at about four years. OpenAlex retrieved October 2026; figure date 2026-10-27."
